  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1244 PM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nThrough early next week:\n\nHigh pressure will continue to be the dominant feature this week, \nsupporting a continuation of warm and dry conditions. Deep mixing \nwill allow for low RH to fall in the 20s and 30s. Despite fairly \nlight winds, will maintain elevated fire danger in light of the very \ndry fuels and burn bans in many counties. Moisture will increase \nahead of an expected shortwave Friday into Saturday which will limit \nfire danger, or even eliminate it for the weekend.\n\nThe aforementioned shortwave associated with the subtropical jet \nwill be our next weather maker, and our source of rainfall for the\nfirst time in many areas in several weeks. It won't be a washout \nor drought busting, but may moisten fuels and provide short term \nrelief. Total rainfall is forecast to be between half and one inch\nfor most of the area with rain ending Sunday afternoon. Dry \nweather returns for early next week.\n\nThe polar jet aids in lee cyclogenesis off the Rockies early next \nweek and at some point phases will subtropical jet energy, though \nguidance is varied on timing of this. The surface low will drag a \ncold front across our area around the Tuesday time frame and may \ngive additional rain chances, however, confidence is low. /SAS/",
